Chaga (Inonotus obliquus) doesn't look like an ordinary mushroom. It is a black, cracked growth on the bark of birch trees with a golden-orange interior. In Siberia, Scandinavia and the Baltics people have drunk it instead of tea for centuries.

## What makes it special?

Chaga grows on living birch for years and stores compounds from the bark — including betulin. It also contains large amounts of melanin and polyphenols, making it one of the natural products with the highest antioxidant activity.

## Three ways to prepare Chaga

- Classic decoction: 1 tablespoon of Chaga in 1 litre of water, simmered for 1 hour
- Thermos method: pour 60–70°C water over it and leave overnight — this preserves more of the active compounds
- Chaga latte: add warm almond milk, cinnamon and a spoon of honey to a strong decoction

> You can simmer the same Chaga chunks 2–3 times, until the brew turns pale.

## Who should avoid it?

Chaga contains oxalates, so people with kidney stones should avoid it. Caution is also needed with blood-thinning and blood-sugar-lowering medication. If in doubt, ask your doctor.
